Managing Threads
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Once we choose to use concurrency, we will typically need the ability to launch threads, wait for
them to finish, run in the background, pass parameters, transfer ownership...
It'll also be useful to have an eye for the number the threads our application might use

Every C++ program has atleast one thread, referred to as the main thread. This is the thread
running the function `main()`. From this thread, we can launch other threads.
Threads run until they return, at which point they stop.
Threads can be initialized with any C++ `callable` type, that is, any entity that can be invoked
using the function-call syntax `()`
<c++>
#include <thread>
#include <iostream>
struct callableStruct{
    void operator()()const{
        std::cout << "Ran Callable Struct" << std::endl;
    }
};

int main(){
    callableStruct s{};
    std::thread t(s);
    t.join();
}
</c++>
Note that the Object is *copied* into the storage of the thread, thus it is expected that the copy
behavior is equivalent to the original for clarity. 

Note that the following, where we pass a temporary rather than a named variable, --
<c++>
#include <thread>
#include <iostream>
struct callableStruct{
    void operator()()const{
        std::cout << "Ran Callable Struct" << std::endl;
    }
};

int main(){
    callableStruct s{};
    std::thread t(callableStruct());
}
</c++>
-- compiles and achieves completion, but does not actually launch a thread. In actuality, adding
`t.join()` at the end causes the program to fail compilation. In reality, the code is compiling 
to the following:

    std::thread t(callableStruct());
       ▲        ▲          ▲
       │        │          │
       └─Return └─Function └─Parameter
           Type       Name        Type

This can be avoided by using
    a) std::thread t((callableStruct()));   // extra set of parantheses
    b) std::thread t{callableStruct()};     // uniform initialization

A type of callable object that completely avoids this issue is the lambda expression
<c++>
#include <thread>
#include <iostream>
    int main(){
        std::thread t([]{std::cout << "Ran λ" << std::endl;});
        t.join();
    }
</c++>

 λ
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
The simplest lamba expression defines a self-contained function that takes no paramaters, and
relies only on global variables and functions. It is not required to have a return value.
A lambda expression may take the following form:

    []{                                 // begin the λ expression with `[]`
        std::cout << "λ expression\n";
    }();                                // finish the λ and call it

the previous example is quite silly, as it takes no reference to any state, no input paramaters,
performs an immutable operation, and is immediately called. The lambda function could logically be
replaced with simply inligning the code:
    std::cout << "λ expression\n";
A more interesting example may take the following form:
<c++>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
int main(){
    std::vector<int> data = {1, 2, 3, 5, 8};
    std::for_each(data.begin(), data.end(), [](int i){std::cout << i << ", ";});
    std::cout << std::endl;
}
</c++>

The λ above is passed to std::for_each as a callable object, and uses a parameter list declared
after its lambda introducer, [].

Return values can be implied by having a return statement inside your lambda, e.g.

<c++>
#include <iostream>
int main(){
    std::cout << "Lambda returned: " << [](){
        std::cout <<"<λ returns implicit Integer> " ;
        return 42;
    }() << std::endl;
}
</c++>

prints out the return value of 42.

Here is a more complicated (but slightly more useful) example
<c++>
#include <mutex>
#include <thread>
#include <condition_variable>
int main(){
    std::condition_variable cond;
};
</c++>
